{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And now, behold, the cry, etc.</b> One must know why He repeated what was said, since He already said ״ואת צעקתם״ etc. There is further a fine point in His saying <b>and now.</b> Further, His saying <b>behold.</b> Further, His saying <b>has come to me</b> — for there was no need to say ״to me.״ Further, His saying <b>and I have also seen</b> — since He already said this above." "It appears that HaShem was informing Moses that then, while He was yet speaking with him, the cry of the children of Israel came anew. Therefore He was precise in saying <b>and now</b>, and also said <b>behold</b>, as though showing him and saying: behold, it is before me." "And He said <b>has come to me</b>, because there are gradations among the prayers brought before the One who answers: there are some that the ministering angels bring and present before Him, blessed be He, and there are some that are of such elevation that they possess the power of needing no intermediary to reach before the Creator — rather they themselves approach before Him. Therefore He said <b>has come to me</b>, meaning, without an intermediary." "And His saying <b>and I have also seen the oppression</b> — according to the words of the Tanna (in the Passover Haggadah) who said that this — the oppression — is a particular that was not specified in the preceding words of HaShem, HaShem needed to state it. And He intended in saying <b>I have seen</b> to magnify the intensity of the desire for redemption, as He explained in the verse (Exodus 2:25) ״וידע אלהים״. And the intention in informing all this is to say <b>and now, come</b> etc., meaning: immediately and at once, without any delay — because of the distress they are under, one must not wait any time at all."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:57.654554833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}